import Bitcoin.Numeric.Interface

/-!
# Y04 property-test corpus — Numeric/ScriptNum (`CScriptNum` encode/decode)

2026-10-16
(spec/proofs/BitcoinProofs/Property/Y04/, mirroring Property/Y01 style).

Ground truth: Bitcoin Core `src/script/script.h` @ `cce32b168`
(`CScriptNum::serialize`, `set_vch`, the decode constructor, `getint`)
and `src/script/interpreter.cpp` @ `cce32b168` (`CastToBool`), fetched
from github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin at that pinned commit.  The corpus is
generated by a plain-python reference transcribed VERBATIM from that
source (a plain-python reference, LCG seed
20261016, REPRODUCIBLE) and is independent of the Lean implementation.

Every case is a kernel `decide`-checkable concrete vector, so this
module building green IS the Lean-vs-python agreement check; any
divergence fails the build naming the offending example.

Sections (432 decide-assertions over 376 generated cases):
  A   canonical `encodeScriptNum` vs python `serialize` (edges)
  A2  encoder ignores the `minimal` flag (Core: unconditionally canonical)
  B   `decodeScriptNum` verdicts vs python (minimal true/false, width 4)
  B2  width-5 decode (CLTV/CSV operand width)
  C   negative-zero / sign-magnitude corners (`-0`, `0x80..`)
  D   roundtrip `decode (encode v) = some v` iff |v| <= 2^31-1
  E   `castToBool` incl. the lone-`0x80` negative-zero caveat
  F   `getIntClamped` int32 clamping (SCR-086)
  G   pseudo-random values, seed 20261016 (encode+roundtrip+getint)
  H   pseudo-random wire vectors, seed 20261016 (full-verdict equality)

No Lean-vs-python mismatch was found.
-/

